ff scale`.\n\nWhen a game fails, every experiment containing it is scored as failed: lower-is-better metrics are set to `1e6` and `min_margin` to `-1e6`. Metrics are recomputed from the payoffs and the submitted distribution, never taken from numbers the method reports.\n\n## Primary Target\n\n### `biased_shapley_augmented`\n\nWhat it tests:\n- Whether the method avoids inventing a strict order in cyclic games. The public game is the paper's Table 2 game with R, P, S and N. The hidden games are transformed and populated cyclic games whose deviation ratings are provably all equal.\n\nPrimary metric:\n- `dev_gap` (lower is better): largest spread of the first cyclic player's ratings. `0` means the ratings are equal.\n\nAdditional scored checks:\n- `all_player_dev_gap` (lower is better): spread across both cyclic players' actions.\n- `player_symmetry_gap` (lower is better): largest same-strategy mismatch between the two players.\n- `CCE_gap` (lower is better): recomputed total CCE regret of the submitted distribution. It is 0 for every output that passes the gates.\n\n## Constraint Targets\n\n### `clone_invariance`\n\nWhat it tests:\n- Whether duplicating a strategy leaves every original rating unchanged and gives the clone its source's rating.\n\nPrimary metric:\n- `max_diff` (lower is better): largest clone-induced change in the ratings of the other players' strategies, the ones facing the clone.\n\nAdditional scored checks:\n- `all_players_max_diff` (lower is better): largest change over all original actions of all players.\n- `clone_source_gap` (lower is better): clone's rating versus its source's.\n\n### `mixture_invariance`\n\nWhat it tests:\n- Whether adding a mixed strategy, with weights fixed by the evaluator, leaves all original ratings unchanged and gives the new strategy the corresponding mixed rating.\n\nPrimary metrics:\n- `original_max_diff` (lower is better): largest change in any original rating, all players.\n- `mixture_error` (lower is better): mixture's rating minus the same mixture of the original ratings.\n\nAdditional scored check:\n- `max_CCE_gap` (lower is better): worst recomputed CCE regret of the base and augmented games.\n\n### `offset_invariance`\n\nWhat it tests:\n- Whether adding arbitrary offsets `b_p(a_-p)`, fixed by the evaluator, leaves every rating unchanged.\n\nPrimary metric:\n- `max_diff` (lower is better): largest absolute (uncentered) change of any rating.\n\nAdditional scored check:\n- `max_CCE_gap` (lower is better): worst recomputed CCE regret of the base and offset games.\n\n## Secondary Property Target\n\n### `dominance_preserving`\n\nWhat it tests:\n- Whether a strictly dominant strategy is rated above the strategy it dominates. The evaluator derives the dominance pairs from the game itself.\n\nPrimary metric:\n- `dominance_violations` (lower is better): number of strictly dominated pairs whose rating advantage is not positive.\n\nAdditional scored checks:\n- `CCE_gap` (lower is better): recomputed total CCE regret.\n- `min_margin` (higher is better, at most 1): smallest ratio of rating advantage to guaranteed payoff advantage. Every game contains a constant-gap pair, on which any rating that is the deviation gain of a distribution scores exactly 1. A larger rating separation cannot raise it, and a reversed order lowers it.\n\n## Baselines\n\n`uniform_payoff` is scored in every experiment. `zero_rating` and `negative_uniform_payoff` are scored in dominance. All three are computed by the evaluator on the same games.\n\n## Ablation Targets\n\nNo separate ablation experiment is packaged in `scoring/scores.json`. The hardening audit (`HARDENING.md`, `redteam/diagnostics.json`) records what happens without the sequential freezing step: a single min-max LP, any CCE, or the earlier implementation's freeze-every-tight-constraint rule. All three fail the hidden invariance checks, but they are diagnostics, not scored experiments.\n\nThe benchmark contains exactly the five scored experiments above, which correspond one-to-one with `scoring/scores.json`.\n# Scientist Constraints\n\nThis is a game-theoretic rating benchmark, not a predictive-model training task.\n\n## Method Interface\n\nThe method is the rating program `scripts/rate.sh GAMES_JSON RATINGS_JSON`. The contract is in [briefing/evaluation.md](/home/user/briefing/evaluation.md). For every game it must return:\n- one finite real rating per action of every player, and\n- the joint distribution those ratings come from: a probability vector over joint actions, C order.\n\nThe distribution must be a coarse correlated equilibrium, and each rating must equal that distribution's deviation gain for the action. Outputs that break this contract are scored as failed rather than ranked.\n\n## Data\n\nThe evaluators supply the scored games; the method does not choose or edit them:\n- `bash scripts/evaluate.sh` and `bash scripts/evaluate_train.sh` use the five documented public games. `data/game_data.py` contains the same definitions for development use.\n- `bash scripts/evaluate_test.sh` generates its own hidden games at test time. It reads nothing from `data/`, `checkpoints/`, `baseline/` or `scoring/reference.json`.\n\nNo external datasets are needed or used.\n\n## Evaluation Protocol\n\nThe scientist must keep the five scored experiments and their entrypoints:\n- `biased_shapley_augmented`, `clone_invariance`, `mixture_invariance`, `offset_invariance`, `dominance_preserving`\n- `bash scripts/evaluate.sh` (standard), `bash scripts/evaluate_train.sh`, `bash scripts/evaluate_test.sh` (hidden, no arguments)\n\n`eval/` and `scripts/evaluate*.sh` belong to the benchmark. Baselines, transformations, mixture weights, offsets, dominance pairs, tolerances and the test size are all fixed there.\n\n## Method Boundary\n\nThe scientist may improve or replace the implementation behind `scripts/rate.sh`, but the result must remain a principled strategy-rating method for normal-form games, not a fit to the benchmark outputs.\n\nThe scientist may NOT:\n- Hardcode the benchmark's target numbers or special-case recognizable games\n- Ignore the game structure and score the experiments with table-specific rules\n- Change the scored game definitions, evaluators, tolerances or baselines while still claiming improvement on this benchmark\n- Change the meaning of `dev_gap`, `CCE_gap`, `max_diff`, `mixture_error`, `dominance_violations` or `min_margin`\n- Skip any scored experiment, or read or modify the hidden evaluator from the rating program\n\n## Compliance Checklist\n- [ ] `scripts/rate.sh` rates any finite 2- or 3-player normal-form game it is given (including players with a single action)\n- [ ] Ratings are the deviation gains of the returned CCE\n- [ ] All five scored experiments are evaluated through the provided entrypoints\n- [ ] Clone, mixture, offset and dominance properties hold on the hidden games, not only the public ones\n# Research Direction\n\n## Research Problem\n\nThe benchmark studies how to rate strategies in multi-player, general-sum normal-form games when interactions are strategic rather than purely transitive. The core challenge is to produce ratings that remain meaningful when there are cycles, mixed motives, redundant strategies, mixed-strategy extensions, and payoff offsets.\n\n## Scope\n\nScientists should work on principled game-theoretic rating methods for normal-form games that preserve the invariance and dominance properties emphasized by the paper. Work that merely fits a scalar ordering to the final benchmark outputs, without modeling the strategic game structure, is out of scope.\n\n## Approach Scope\n\nThe scientist may explore:\n- Better optimization or numerical methods for computing deviation-style ratings\n- Alternative principled rating formulations for the same problem family\n- Improved scalability to larger games while preserving the scored properties\n- Broader evaluations after keeping the scored benchmark intact\n\n## How Work Is Measured\n\nA