-{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgndf1hBVnhWmHubwUBpe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ToCr5QJfcHZw6b1yZeiBpw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('/home/user/briefing/method.md'); t = p.read_text()\nold = '''3. From the LP solution, identify which deviation gain constraints are active (at the optimum)\n4. Freeze those constraints (add them to the active set) with their computed ratings'''\nnew = '''3. From the LP solution, identify which deviation gain constraints are active: those with a nonzero dual variable (Section 4.1). By complementary slackness they are tight at every optimal distribution\n4. Freeze those constraints (add them to the active set) with their computed ratings. A constraint that is tight at the returned vertex but has a zero dual is not frozen yet; if it is truly pinned it is frozen in a later iteration at the same value'''\nassert old in t; t = t.replace(old, new)\nold2 = '''The paper reports using CVXPY with GLOP (OR-Tools' simplex solver) as the backend. This reproduction keeps the same sequential-LP algorithm but implements it with SciPy's `linprog` (HiGHS backend) instead of CVXPY/GLOP.'''\nnew2 = '''The paper reports using CVXPY with GLOP (OR-Tools' simplex solver) as the backend. This reproduction keeps the same sequential-LP algorithm but implements it with SciPy's `linprog` (HiGHS backend) instead of CVXPY/GLOP (`method/deviation_rating.py`). The benchmark calls it through the rating program `scripts/rate.sh` -> `method/rate.py`, which returns, for every game, the ratings and the final LP's distribution, under which every rating is exactly the corresponding deviation gain.\n\nAn earlier version of this implementation also froze every constraint that happened to be numerically tight at the vertex the solver returned. When the optimal face is not a single point, for example when one player's best action