-{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgndx3RasF3prmcWH8xfj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The gym is hardened. None of the 51 shortcuts beats the reference any more: the 45 from REDTEAM plus 6 new ones aimed at the new design. Before this, five beat it and about twenty tied it. The reference itself still scores exactly 0 on every invariance metric.\n\n**What changed in the test**\n- **Methods run on games they haven't seen.** A method is now a program, `scripts/rate.sh`. The hidden test builds 186 games from a seed that exists only in `eval/test/evaluate.py`, gives them anonymous ids, shuffles them, and runs the program on them.\n- **Outputs have to be genuine.** Every number must be a finite real, the equilibrium must sum to 1 and be a CCE, and the ratings must equal that equilibrium's deviation gains. Any failure scores the whole experiment as failed. All metrics are recomputed from the payoffs and the submitted equilibrium; anything else the method reports is ignored.\n- **The evaluator owns the comparison.** The evaluator fixes mixture weights, offsets, clone positions, dominance pairs, baselines, tolerances and test size. The checks the old test skipped are back: mixture drift, absolute (not centred) offset error, and an equilibrium required for clones. The dominance margin is capped at 1, so it can't be inflated.\n- **The method can't interfere with scoring.** The evaluator runs with `python3 -I`, and the test script ends in `exec`. Every process the method starts, including detached daemons, is killed before scoring. Checkpoint folders are never read.\n- **Bystander players.** I added some games with an extra player whose payoff depends only on its own action. Without them, running just the first step of the paper's algorithm tied the reference.\n\n**I changed the reproduced method itself.** The implementation froze every constraint that happened to be tight at the solver's answer, which is not the paper's rule. It failed the hidden invariance checks on 0 of 11 seed suites passed. I restored the paper's rule (Section 4.1: freeze constraints with nonzero duals). With it the reference passes all 11 suites and still reproduces the paper's Table 2 (all four ratings −2720/964). The old rule is kept only as a diagnostic.\n\n**Final numbers**\n- **Reference:** 0 on every metric, `min_margin` 1, in 3 identical runs and across 11 seed suites. `scoring/scores_test.json`, `scores_train.json` and `scores.json` were regenerated from these runs.\n- **Uniform baseline:** now worse than the reference on every experiment. Before, the hidden offset check gave it the same 0 as the reference.\n- **The 4 shortcuts that tie and the 2 probes that tie** all run the honest method underneath: ignored dominance pairs, a 4e-9 perturbation, decoy checkpoints, a decoy method id, a daemon that was killed before it could rewrite scores, and a command appended to the test script that never ran.\n- **The cheap alternatives fail every suite.** Running only the first step of the paper's algorithm, taking any CCE, or taking the first pure Nash equilibrium leaves largest errors of 3.7 to 7.9. Deviation gains at the uniform distribution fail outright because they are not an equilibrium.\n\n**What I couldn't fix**\n- **Nothing can beat the reference.** It already sits at the optimum, so the gym rewards correctness, not improvement.\n- **Algorithm not enforced.** The test checks the paper's properties, not its algorithm. A different method that removes clones and mixtures before picking a CCE could tie; I treat that as a legitimate alternative rating rule.\n- **The method can read the hidden test.** It runs while `eval/test/evaluate.py` (seed included) is on disk. This only helps that kind of tie, and closing it needs OS-level sandboxing this environment doesn't have.\n- **The container is trusted.** It is built from the agent-editable `environment/setup.sh`, so the hidden test should run in the original image.\n\nHARDENING.md has the per-shortcut fix and before/after scores. I updated the docs future agents read: `briefing/`, `scoring/`, DETAILS.md, DESIGN_NOTES.md, PROGRESS.md, the redteam audit protocol, and the milestone log. `README.md` and `manifest.json` are character-device files, so I couldn't update them. `scripts/run.sh` reproduces the reference end to end, and `redteam/hardening_job.sh` reruns the whole audit. Everything is committed and the milestone stays `secondary_claims`.\n\n```yaml\naction: done\nnotes: \"Hardened: rating-program contract, hidden games generated at test time, validity gates, isolated evaluator, paper's dual freezing rule; reference 0 on all invariance metrics (11/11 suites); all 51 shortcuts fail or tie only via the honest method (HARDENING.md).\"\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1213,"cache_read_input_tokens":494807,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1213},"output_tokens":682,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6904bb9f-75a3-4eec-b686-cd128c7631eb","uuid":"fcf18caa-974d-464a-85b6-742bd3d19f1e","timestamp":"2026-11-07T00:41:24.052Z","request_id":"req_011Cgndx3DCDauksNPC7zaAr"}
